This application bundles the King James Version (assets/data/kjv_sword.zip) as
distributed by the CrossWire Bible Society, https://www.crosswire.org/sword/ —
the "KJV" module (the KJV2003 Project text, with Strong's numbers and
morphology tagging), whose own module distribution license is GPL (©
CrossWire Bible Society 2003-2023; CrossWire's conf file states: "CrossWire
Bible Society hereby grants a general public license to use this text for any
purpose"). The base 1611/1769 text itself is public domain outside the United
Kingdom; within the UK, Crown Letters Patent restrict commercial printing and
publishing of the KJV to the Queen's/King's Printer, a restriction that does
not apply to this application's free digital distribution. Bundled unmodified
except for the paragraph-break markers described below.

CrossWire's KJV module carries no paragraph markup (its own conf declares
`Feature=NoParagraphs`, true of every KJV edition CrossWire distributes).
Paragraph break positions are derived instead from ph4.org's "AV" MyBible
module (King James Version with cross-references, 1914, from A. J. Holman
Company's "Holman Home Bible" — public domain) by
scripts/build_kjv_paragraphs.dart, and applied to CrossWire's text by verse
coordinate at import time. No text from ph4.org's module is bundled or
distributed — only a list of (book, chapter, verse) positions
(assets/data/kjv_paragraph_breaks.json) marking where a new paragraph begins.
As two independently-digitized editions, the two sources' verse divisions
disagree in one book (Esther); that book is excluded from the paragraph-break
data rather than guessed at, so it renders with no paragraph breaks.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
lib/data/importer/curated_journeys_data.dart contains hand-authored Atlas
journey waypoints (people and places the bundled datasets above don't
otherwise link with enough detail to plot a real route). It cross-references
those datasets by their own identifiers (bible_people.slug from Theographic
Bible Metadata, places.name from Bible-Geocoding-Data) for interoperability,
but its content — which verse establishes each waypoint, and which place it
resolves to — is independently researched against the source text, not
copied or adapted from either bundled dataset. It is licensed under the
Apache License 2.0 along with the rest of the application code.
